{"id":"e2-1-5","num":"1.5","level":"B","minutes":45,"title":"Build it: the attendance summary","summary":"A brief and hidden tests, with no step-by-step instructions: read a week’s attendance sheet, add a Summary sheet with each student’s days present and percentage, save it, and report how many are below 75%.","objectives":["Plan a program before writing it.","Combine reading rows, counting, and writing a new sheet.","Save the result as a new workbook."],"lecture":[{"title":"The brief","slides":[{"h":"Mr Kulkarni’s register","say":"Mr Kulkarni teaches evening computer classes in Pune. His register is a workbook: one row per student, one column per day, P for present and A for absent. Each Friday he needs a summary for the office.","points":["Read the sheet **Week 1** of attendance.xlsx.","Add a sheet **Summary** with **Name, Present, Percent** for every student.","Percent is rounded to a whole number.","Save as **attendance_summary.xlsx** and print **N students, M below 75%**."],"p":["Mr Kulkarni teaches evening computer classes in Pune. His register is a workbook: one row per student, one column per day, P for present and A for absent. Each Friday he needs a summary for the office.","Read the sheet **Week 1** of attendance.xlsx.","Add a sheet **Summary** with **Name, Present, Percent** for every student.","Percent is rounded to a whole number.","Save as **attendance_summary.xlsx** and print **N students, M below 75%**."],"visual":{"type":"pycode","text":"Name   Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri\nAsha    P   P   A   P   P\nRavi    P   A   A   P   P","title":"attendance.xlsx · Week 1"},"real":{"who":"Mr Kulkarni","text":"teaches evening computer classes and keeps a register for the office.","icon":"👨‍🏫"}},{"h":"Two helpful tricks","say":"A row tuple can be sliced like a list, and tuples can count things.","points":["`row[0]` is the name; `row[1:]` is every day after it.","`row[1:].count(\"P\")` counts the P’s.","The number of days is `ws.max_column - 1`, so the same program works for a 6-day week."],"p":["A row tuple can be sliced like a list, and tuples can count things.","`row[0]` is the name; `row[1:]` is every day after it.","`row[1:].count(\"P\")` counts the P’s.","The number of days is `ws.max_column - 1`, so the same program works for a 6-day week."],"visual":{"type":"pycode","text":"row = (\"Asha\", \"P\", \"P\", \"A\", \"P\", \"P\")\nprint(row[0], row[1:].count(\"P\"))","out":"Asha 4"}}],"check":{"q":"How do you count the days present in `row = (\"Ravi\", \"P\", \"A\", \"A\", \"P\", \"P\")`?","options":["`row.count(\"P\")`","`row[1:].count(\"P\")`","`len(row)`","`row[1]`"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both work here, but skipping the name is safer: a student called “P” would be counted."}}],"demo":{"replay":true,"intro":"Watch the plan become the first part of the program: reading and counting, before any writing.","steps":[{"type":"import openpyxl\nwb = openpyxl.load_workbook(\"attendance.xlsx\")\nws = wb[\"Week 1\"]\nfor row in ws.iter_rows(min_row=2, values_only=True):\n    print(row[0], row[1:].count(\"P\"))\n"},{"run":true,"out":"Asha 4\nRavi 3\nImran 5\nDivya 3\n","stdin":[],"files":{"attendance.xlsx":{"blob":"585f2c8378"}},"changed":[]},{"say":"The counting works. The rest (the Summary sheet, the percentage, saving) is yours in the lab."}],"outro":"Now the lab: plan it, then build it.","final":"import openpyxl\nwb = openpyxl.load_workbook(\"attendance.xlsx\")\nws = wb[\"Week 1\"]\nfor row in ws.iter_rows(min_row=2, values_only=True):\n    print(row[0], row[1:].count(\"P\"))\n","sample":[],"minutes":4,"lines":7,"files":{"attendance.xlsx":{"blob":"585f2c8378"}}},"lab":{"py":true,"intro":"Plan first, then write it. Hidden tests use other registers: more students, different weeks.","tasks":[{"id":"plan","kind":"plan","prompt":"Put the steps of the attendance summary in order.","steps":["Open attendance.xlsx and pick the Week 1 sheet","Work out how many days the week has","Add a Summary sheet with the header Name, Present, Percent","For each student: count the P’s, work out the percent, append a row","Count the students below 75%","Save as attendance_summary.xlsx and print the totals"],"explain":"Read, prepare the new sheet, loop, then save and report.","hints":[],"standalone":false},{"id":"build","kind":"write","prompt":"Build it. **attendance.xlsx** has a sheet **Week 1**: a header (**Name** then one column per day) and one row per student, with **P** or **A** for each day. Add a sheet **Summary** to the same workbook with the header **Name, Present, Percent** and one row per student (Percent = present ÷ days × 100, rounded to a whole number). Save it as **attendance_summary.xlsx**, then print **N students, M below 75%**, like **4 students, 2 below 75%**.","model":"import openpyxl\nwb = openpyxl.load_workbook(\"attendance.xlsx\")\nws = wb[\"Week 1\"]\ndays = ws.max_column - 1\nsummary = wb.create_sheet(\"Summary\")\nsummary.append([\"Name\", \"Present\", \"Percent\"])\nlow = 0\nfor row in ws.iter_rows(min_row=2, values_only=True):\n    name = row[0]\n    present = row[1:].count(\"P\")\n    pct = round(present / days * 100)\n    summary.append([name, present, pct])\n    if pct < 75:\n        low += 1\nwb.save(\"attendance_summary.xlsx\")\nprint(f\"{ws.max_row - 1} students, {low} below 75%\")","cases":[{"files":{"attendance.xlsx":{"blob":"585f2c8378"}}},{"files":{"attendance.xlsx":{"blob":"102055af14"}}},{"files":{"attendance.xlsx":{"blob":"b0e7935e15"}}}],"explain":"Count with row[1:].count(\"P\"), days from max_column - 1, then append, save and report.","hints":["days = ws.max_column - 1","summary = wb.create_sheet(\"Summary\")","round(present / days * 100)"],"start":"","standalone":false,"need":["import:openpyxl","for"],"sample":[],"checkFiles":["attendance_summary.xlsx"],"files":{"attendance.xlsx":{"blob":"585f2c8378"}}}]},"quiz":[{"q":"Why work out the days with `ws.max_column - 1` instead of writing 5?","options":["It’s shorter","The same program then works for a 6-day week","max_column is faster","Python needs it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hidden tests use other weeks."},{"q":"What does this print?","code":"row = (\"Divya\", \"A\", \"P\", \"P\", \"A\", \"P\")\nprint(round(row[1:].count(\"P\") / 5 * 100))","py":true,"options":["60","3","40","0.6"],"answer":0,"explain":"3 of 5 days.","type":"predict"},{"q":"Where does `wb.save(...)` go?","options":["Inside the loop","After the loop","Before the loop"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once, when every row is written."},{"q":"`row[1:]` is…","options":["every value after the first","only the second value","the first value","the row number"],"answer":0,"explain":"A slice from index 1 to the end."}],"bank":[{"q":"What does this print?","code":"row = (\"Imran\", \"P\", \"P\", \"P\", \"P\", \"P\")\nprint(row[1:].count(\"A\"))","py":true,"options":["0","5","1"],"answer":0,"explain":"No A’s.","type":"predict"},{"q":"Summary rows are added with…","options":["`summary.append([...])`","`summary.add(...)`","`wb.append(...)`"],"answer":0,"explain":"append adds a row."}],"recap":{"points":["Plan first: read, prepare the new sheet, loop, save, report.","Slices work on row tuples: `row[1:]`; so does `.count()`.","Work sizes out from the sheet (max_column, max_row), so the program works for any week."],"cheat":"present = row[1:].count(\"P\")","cards":[{"id":"e2-1-5a","type":"concept","front":"Count P’s in a row after the name","back":"`row[1:].count(\"P\")`"},{"id":"e2-1-5b","type":"concept","front":"Days in the week from the sheet","back":"`ws.max_column - 1`"}]},"practice":[],"free":true,"module":1,"downloads":[],"shots":[],"blobs":{"585f2c8378":{"b64":"","size":5018,"preview":{"sheets":[{"name":"Week 1","rows":[["Name","Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri"],["Asha","P","P","A","P","P"],["Ravi","P","A","A","P","P"],["Imran","P","P","P","P","P"],["Divya","A","P","P","A","P"]],"maxRow":5,"maxCol":6,"fmt":{"1,1":{"b":1},"1,2":{"b":1},"1,3":{"b":1},"1,4":{"b":1},"1,5":{"b":1},"1,6":{"b":1}},"widths":{},"freeze":null}],"active":0}},"102055af14"